00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:09.230
Tamzin: We look at kind of, the
background it is important because if

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:12.930
you're getting someone who's coming
from -  and question their own identity

00:00:12.950 --> 00:00:17.390
and they've not got supportive parents and
they're not getting the support at home.

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:21.110
Then it becomes, they become
more vulnerable to kind of doing

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:25.280
self-harm or kind of making a, you
know, a kind of impulsive decision.

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:28.270
I've been working with young people
that have suffered,  you know, like

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:32.360
kind of a bad experience, whether it
be an assault, or whether it being

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:37.000
abuse or, you know, we find these young
people, you know, like a long time

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.440
after they're still dealing with it.

00:00:38.489 --> 00:00:42.030
Fran: There's a tendency to
believe that all, all mental

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:44.040
health comes from bad experiences.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.240
And I don't, I don't believe that.

00:00:45.301 --> 00:00:46.771
But I do think it can be.

00:00:46.788 --> 00:00:50.057
So I think if we talk again about
care leavers, who I work with a lot.

00:00:50.104 --> 00:00:53.554
There's a high prevalence of mental health
in care leavers, you know, much more

00:00:53.554 --> 00:00:58.504
than in general society because they've
experienced trauma and they've experienced

00:00:58.553 --> 00:01:00.443
loss at an early age and stuff like that.

00:01:00.443 --> 00:01:01.553
So I think it definitely can be.

00:01:02.113 --> 00:01:08.113
Tamzin: They might be a young person who's
LGBQT and they question their identity.

00:01:08.503 --> 00:01:11.773
They might basically, they
might've just come out to friends.

00:01:11.803 --> 00:01:15.373
So that can be a massive risk factor
if they've not got supportive parents.

00:01:15.883 --> 00:01:19.933
And then you might be a young person whose
family have got mental health issues.

00:01:20.408 --> 00:01:25.258
And to us we look at that as a red
flag as well, because if your family

00:01:25.268 --> 00:01:28.478
has got the mental health issues then
obviously that's hard for the young

00:01:28.478 --> 00:01:30.208
person to get support from the family.

00:01:30.448 --> 00:01:33.478
Fran: Childhood experiences,
they call them ACEs.

00:01:33.478 --> 00:01:37.277
So it's Adverse Childhood Experiences,
there's a, there's a sort of theoretical

00:01:37.277 --> 00:01:40.137
view that if you've had two or three
ACEs - I can't remember the exact

00:01:40.157 --> 00:01:44.181
number - that is likely to lead to
mental health issues as you get older.

00:01:44.181 --> 00:01:49.741
And I certainly believe that, but
I also believe that you can have a

00:01:49.741 --> 00:01:53.461
perfectly fine childhood and still
develop mental health conditions.

00:01:53.821 --> 00:01:56.491
I certainly think it does play a
role, but I certainly don't think

00:01:56.491 --> 00:02:01.801
we should think that it's the only,
the only factor and that if someone

00:02:01.801 --> 00:02:05.453
doesn't have any childhood, you know,
difficult experiences in childhood,

00:02:05.453 --> 00:02:07.463
that they're going to be fine for the
rest of their life 'cause they might

00:02:07.463 --> 00:02:09.533
well experience it through over reasons.

00:02:09.683 --> 00:02:12.233
Seemingly everything's fine in their
life, their parents are together, they've

00:02:12.233 --> 00:02:13.613
got a happy home, they're well off.

00:02:14.163 --> 00:02:17.058
There's no financial issues, all
that kind of thing then and then they

00:02:17.568 --> 00:02:18.828
struggle with depression, anxiety.

00:02:18.828 --> 00:02:20.628
People can sort of write it
